how_to_use:
Reference only, as a design model for building our own LOTRO capture tool. Do not depend on it or port it. Reasons: (1) The code has no declared license, so copying it is not permitted, and it links AGPL ACE DLLs. (2) It is Windows-only WinForms. (3) Every protocol definition (opcodes, message structures, port range) is for AC1. LOTRO's protocol is related to AC1 but differs, and we have not verified how far. Ideas worth reproducing clean-room in our own LOTRO packet tool:
- the pipeline: a pcap/pcapng reader, then a UDP filter by server port range, then a transport-header parser, then fragment reassembly, then per-opcode message decoders shown as a tree;
- a "scraper" plugin framework that runs batch analyses over large capture collections;
- a separate protocol-description repo (ACE pulls XML/docs from acemulator.github.io releases), which suggests we keep our LOTRO protocol spec as data separate from the tool.
In practice this means Wireshark plus a Lua or Rust dissector, or a small Rust/Python CLI, written by us. The AC transport-header layout below is a starting hypothesis to test against real LOTRO captures. It is not a fact about LOTRO.

Use as reference only, through clean-room spec extraction. Do not port it and do not depend on it. The repo has no license, so it is all rights reserved, and copying or porting its code into our project is not permitted. Its build also needs the author's other artifacts, which are not on Maven Central (and the memory and DAT-reader repos are not public at the URLs we tried). What it is worth to us is facts, which can be written down as an independent spec:
- the named game properties that make up player state, and their value types;
- the WLib ClassInstance attribute-ID scheme and a few concrete attribute IDs for TravelRecord, quest records and legendary data;
- the DatPosition and landblock/cell/zone addressing;
- 32-bit Unix-seconds timestamps.

This is useful for designing our runtime entity/property model (a client-side WState/PropertiesSet equivalent) and for checking our property-dictionary decoding against known names. It is not a source for the memory layout or the network protocol, because it has neither. The underlying property names and IDs come from the user's own DAT files, which our DAT reader will decode directly. Correction to the claim: the license is not Apache-2.0 (none is declared). The repo extracts from already-decoded PropertiesSet and ClassInstance objects; it does not read client memory or WState, which is the job of the separate delta-lotro-memory library.

key_facts:
Checked by fetching the GitHub page, the commits page and raw Program.cs through WebFetch. A local clone was not possible because the scratch disk was full (ENOSPC). Parsed layout of a client_local text-table file (DID class 0x25xxxxxx), as Program.cs reads it:
- Header: int32 file id (DID), then int32 unknown (always 1), then byte (type/flag), then a count encoded as a 1-or-2-byte "TSize" varint: if the first byte has 0x80 set, len = ((b0 ^ 0x80) << 8) | b1.
- Entries repeat until EOF. Each entry is:
  - an 8-byte key, read as an int64 "hash" (in practice an int32 token/label id plus an int32 unknown);
  - int32 piece count, then that many strings. Each string is a TSize varint giving the length in UTF-16 code units, followed by len*2 bytes of UTF-16LE (Encoding.Unicode);
  - int32 argument-reference count, then that many int32s;
  - a byte giving the number of variable groups. Each group is an int32 count followed by that many strings in the same varint+UTF-16 encoding.
- Output lines look like "{FileID}{Index:X2} - {text}", with a long line of '#' characters between files.
- No error handling and no knowledge of the DAT container. The help text only says "Please move this programme into the folder with localization .bin files!"
- This layout agrees with the text-table format documented elsewhere (for example lotro-tools / LotroCompanion), so the two can cross-check each other.
